WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
We have to recognize that the classroom
is not the only place to learn.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:11.070
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann and today I wanna
talk about, you know, why you are the most

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:15.330
important person in language learning:
don't compare yourself to others.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.830
Don't worry about others.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:21.510
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.130
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:23.535 --> 00:00:27.195
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:27.765 --> 00:00:31.935
So, uh, you may have seen the video
that I put up where I tried to speak

00:00:31.935 --> 00:00:35.145
20 languages, uh, sort of in one shot.

00:00:35.895 --> 00:00:41.085
Um, obviously I messed up, uh, but
part of the purpose in doing that

00:00:41.085 --> 00:00:44.565
was to show that we are going to
mess up when we speak languages.

00:00:44.985 --> 00:00:49.815
It was pointed out for example, and I
immediately noticed that in Italian,

00:00:49.815 --> 00:00:53.145
I said ... instead of ...  okay.

00:00:53.715 --> 00:00:57.495
Easy enough to confuse
two romance languages.

00:00:58.005 --> 00:01:04.155
Once more, I noticed that in my Korean, I
think I used ... which is very in Persian

00:01:04.455 --> 00:01:10.725
and I started to use ... which is much
or many in Persian, again, in my Korean.

00:01:11.895 --> 00:01:14.085
I can't remember all the other
places where I messed up.

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:18.960
if I thought about it, I would, but
of course, when I see that I would

00:01:18.965 --> 00:01:22.620
like to redo it, but if I redo it,
I'll probably make a mistake somewhere

00:01:23.804 --> 00:01:26.505
and this is a bit like when we use
the language that we're learning.

00:01:26.505 --> 00:01:30.375
Sometimes we, we get it right one time and
then we don't get it right the next time.

00:01:30.435 --> 00:01:34.125
And there are always people
who speak better than we do.

00:01:34.455 --> 00:01:35.655
None of that matters.

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:37.845
You know, the brain is
gonna go where it goes.

00:01:37.935 --> 00:01:40.965
Sometimes it'll go to the
right spot and it'll find the

00:01:40.965 --> 00:01:42.134
word that you're looking for.

00:01:42.255 --> 00:01:43.365
Sometimes it won't.

00:01:43.365 --> 00:01:45.285
Sometimes it'll go to another language.

00:01:45.615 --> 00:01:49.810
Uh, I've spent so much of the last three
months with Persian, my brain is kind

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.950
of, you know, a little bit twitchy,
uh, when it comes to looking for words,

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.940
it tends to go for a Persian word,
not a problem, you correct yourself.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.760
Or the other person kind of
says, Hmm, what was that?

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.590
And then you continue communicating.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:08.230
So it's very important that we put
ourselves at the, you know, at the sort

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:11.830
of focal point of our learning activity.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:15.110
And as long as we feel that we're,
you know, enjoying the language

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.190
we think we're slowly improving.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.490
Maybe some days we think we're no
longer improving, but we have to

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:24.420
remind ourselves that if we continue
to put in the time, we will improve,

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.910
as long as we engage with the language.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.020
And in that regard, I thought it was
quite interesting the comment that I

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:36.750
received on my, uh, YouTube channel here,
where someone said that, uh, you know,

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.560
I used to be a fan of yours Steve, but
now as I learn more about linguistics,

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:46.620
educational psychology, and language
pedagogy, I realized the videos you

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.870
make and the methodology you promote are
inefficient at best and harmful at worst.

00:02:51.875 --> 00:03:00.300
Poor ideas, just devaluing the classroom,
promoting your program, uh, misleading for

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.010
an audience that doesn't know any better.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:03.990
And this person's a teacher.

00:03:05.295 --> 00:03:05.984
Fine.

00:03:06.435 --> 00:03:10.125
Uh, teachers are gonna defend the
classroom and there are undoubtedly

00:03:10.125 --> 00:03:13.484
people who are good teachers and
people who learn in the classroom.

00:03:13.565 --> 00:03:17.535
However, a lot of people find
the classroom frustrating.

00:03:17.745 --> 00:03:22.185
A lot of people were turned off
language learning by the classroom.

00:03:22.605 --> 00:03:27.075
Uh, at least in Canada, in the
English language school system, people

00:03:27.255 --> 00:03:29.834
by and large don't learn French.

00:03:30.225 --> 00:03:36.055
So we have to recognize that the
classroom is not the only place to learn.

00:03:36.295 --> 00:03:41.995
In fact, I have found that in
trying to measure, you know, who

00:03:41.995 --> 00:03:45.835
succeeds in language learning,
it's those people who do things

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:48.265
outside the classroom that succeed.

00:03:48.385 --> 00:03:53.365
And what I promote here at my
channel is lots of input listening

00:03:53.365 --> 00:03:55.135
and reading, that can't be bad.

00:03:55.315 --> 00:03:59.425
Now, of course, I put in a plug for LingQ
here and there, but LingQ is not the

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.015
only place where you can listen and read.

00:04:01.295 --> 00:04:05.835
But the basic principle that you have
to bring the language into you and that

00:04:05.835 --> 00:04:11.325
until your brain has been exposed to
enough of, to enough of the language, the

00:04:11.329 --> 00:04:13.365
grammar instruction is not going to work.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:17.685
You know, if you're given, uh, you know,
so often when you're given, say road

00:04:17.685 --> 00:04:20.175
instructions and you say yes, yes, yes.

00:04:20.515 --> 00:04:24.284
And then it's completely gone because
you haven't been down that route

00:04:24.284 --> 00:04:27.765
before you haven't been past the
post office to the intersection.

00:04:27.765 --> 00:04:32.610
So until you've had some of that
experience if it's in your neighborhood

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.580
and they say, the store you're looking
for is located besides something

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.670
else and you know, the neighborhood,
then those instructions work.

00:04:38.909 --> 00:04:42.420
But in languages, if you haven't
had enough of the language in you,

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:46.260
so that you're familiar with some
of the ways that the languages,

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.370
you know, the words come together.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.390
The grammar instructions
are not that helpful.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.810
And so linguistics, pedagogy, I have ne...

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:58.550
I have books, I bought books in behind
me on linguistics and teaching pedagogy.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:04.160
I've never found them all that useful
because it's basically a personal journey.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:08.730
If you're interested in the language,
you seek out content of interest,

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.300
a teacher can help you find the
content content of interest.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:16.170
A teacher can stimulate that interest,
but you've gotta be interested.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:19.170
And then you have to put in
the time, it's that simple.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:24.330
It's not a matter of complicated pedagogy
and furthermore for this teacher to

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:29.520
suggest that the people who listen
to me simply don't know any better.

00:05:29.909 --> 00:05:34.830
You know, I've had so many people tell
me, Steve, I never was able to learn a

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:39.990
language in the classroom, since listening
to you I have improved tremendously

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:45.060
since I started reading more, rather than
trying to speak more at an early stage.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.664
Since I started listening more
I have better comprehension.

00:05:48.664 --> 00:05:50.945
And lo and behold, now
I'm able to speak better.

00:05:51.034 --> 00:05:53.075
So many people have said that to me.

00:05:53.465 --> 00:05:57.245
I don't think those people are simply
people who don't know any better.

00:05:57.335 --> 00:06:01.474
However, I understand that teachers
wanna protect what they're doing and

00:06:01.474 --> 00:06:05.135
they want to feel, you know, that
what they're doing is valuable and it

00:06:05.135 --> 00:06:10.435
can be valuable, particularly if the
teacher can motivate the learner to do

00:06:10.435 --> 00:06:13.920
things, you know, on his or her own.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:18.090
You know, and, and unfortunately, like I
mentioned before this language app that

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:23.370
the Canadian government put out called
Mauril or Mauril, uh, where basically

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:30.380
it's people creating comprehension
questions for, you know, Canadian like

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:33.030
CBC or Radio Canada television programs.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.070
We don't need comprehension questions.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.440
We don't need teachers
doing that sort of thing.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:42.390
What we need is an ability to access
that content, put up all the transcripts

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:47.670
to the programs that, uh, CBC and Radio
Canada and people will be able to learn

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:49.840
from those because it's a personal.

00:06:50.455 --> 00:06:54.655
So, and, and we need to compare
ourselves inevitably, some people

00:06:54.655 --> 00:06:56.395
are going do better than others.

00:06:56.484 --> 00:06:58.104
Some people will spend more time.

00:06:58.284 --> 00:07:00.114
Some people will have
better pronunciation.

00:07:00.145 --> 00:07:01.224
It doesn't matter.

00:07:01.315 --> 00:07:03.085
It's always a personal journey.

00:07:03.114 --> 00:07:04.015
It's up to you.

00:07:04.135 --> 00:07:05.034
You're in charge.

00:07:05.034 --> 00:07:06.145
You're steering the boat.

00:07:06.265 --> 00:07:09.145
You go where you want to go
and don't worry about who's

00:07:09.145 --> 00:07:10.405
behind you and who's ahead of.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.320
Or who's trying to wave at you and
tell you, don't go there, go here.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:15.490
It's a personal journey.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:17.140
So there you have it.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.280
That's my take on this idea of, of, you
know, always comparing yourself to others

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:23.800
or, or looking for approval from others.

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:26.590
Don't, it's your own personal journey.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:28.030
Thank you for listening.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:28.930
Bye for now.

